Revision history for Rex-GPU

{{$NEXT}}
  - install_driver on Debian installs a Blackwell GPU's driver (B200/GB200/
    B300, GeForce RTX 50xx, RTX PRO Blackwell, GB10) from NVIDIA's CUDA apt
    repository: cuda-keyring for debian12/debian13 (x86_64, or sbsa on
    arm64), then nvidia-driver-cuda + nvidia-kernel-open-dkms, with Debian
    non-free left alone. It used to install Debian's nvidia-driver (535 on
    bookworm, 550 on trixie). Those versions cannot drive Blackwell, so dpkg
    reported the driver installed but the module never bound. Both open-set
    packages must be ii afterwards. A leftover Debian nvidia-driver no longer
    passes that check. A Blackwell GPU on any other Debian release or
    architecture now dies before the host is touched. Every other GPU on
    Debian is unchanged. Package names and the dependency closure were
    checked against the repository indexes. The install has not run on
    hardware (karr #18)
  - generate_cdi_specs POD: the CDI spec captures the MIG layout at generation
    time; say how to regenerate it after MIG reconfiguration (karr #19)
  - Fix detect() hiding a real GPU behind a virtual display: a single virtio/
    QEMU(QXL)/VMware/VirtualBox display line anywhere in lspci output made the
    whole scan return empty, so a GPU passthrough (vfio-pci) host or cloud GPU
    VM — which shows an emulated console next to the real card — reported no
    NVIDIA GPU and gpu_setup silently did nothing. Virtual displays are now
    skipped per line and the real card is detected; output with only virtual
    displays still returns empty arrays. NVIDIA vGPU guest devices (vendor
    10de) are indistinguishable from passthrough in lspci -nn and are now
    detected the same way. Not hardware-verified (karr #17)
  - configure_containerd (rke2/k3s) now heals an EARLIER containerd clobber: a
    host set up by the 0.001 release carries a stale full-config
    config.toml.tmpl (the bare imports=... + version=2 template, missing the
    Go-template include of the RKE2/K3s base config) that replaced it. RKE2/K3s
    render that stale template, so the generated config.toml already shows an
    nvidia runtime and the "already-wired" no-op that karr #9 added left the
    clobber — missing SystemdCgroup / pinned sandbox image / certs.d — in place.
    configure_containerd now detects that exact bare-clobber signature and
    removes the template before trusting the generated config, then writes the
    additive v3 drop-in; the distribution regenerates its native config on the
    next restart. The match is deliberately narrow (a new pure, offline-testable
    predicate _is_rke2_clobber_tmpl, new t/70-containerd-heal.t): it never
    removes the base-extending config.toml.tmpl, a user's own custom tmpl, or
    config-v3.toml.d/. It does NOT restart rke2/k3s (that would bounce the
    node's containerd and workloads) — it warns that the operator must restart
    the service or reboot for the native config to regenerate; config.toml is
    not self-healing. NB: not hardware-verified — no clobbered node was
    available and the containerd step never ran live (the pipeline died at the
    driver step, karr #7). A maintainer must reproduce a node clobbered by old
    0.002 against modern RKE2 (containerd 2.x / config v3), run configure_containerd,
    restart RKE2, and confirm the regenerated config.toml carries SystemdCgroup,
    the pinned sandbox image and the certs.d config_path plus the nvidia runtime
    (karr #13)
  - generate_cdi_specs no longer writes a static /etc/cdi/nvidia.yaml when a
    managed CDI source already owns the runtime scan dir. Modern
    nvidia-container-toolkit ships nvidia-cdi-refresh.path/.service, which keep
    /run/cdi/nvidia.yaml fresh across driver updates; /etc/cdi and /run/cdi are
    both default CDI scan dirs, so the old always-write behaviour defined
    nvidia.com/gpu twice when the managed source was present (risking a
    duplicate-device load error in CDI consumers) and the static copy drifted
    against the refreshed one. When the nvidia-cdi-refresh unit is installed or
    armed (or a /run/cdi/nvidia.yaml already exists), the managed generator is
    triggered once and left to own CDI; otherwise /etc/cdi is written as before.
    The decision is the pure, offline-testable predicate
    _cdi_managed_source_present (new t/60-cdi-managed-source.t). NB: not
    hardware-verified — the toolkit/CDI step never ran live (the pipeline died
    at the driver step, karr #7); a maintainer must confirm on a real node with
    nvidia-cdi-refresh installed that /etc/cdi stays empty, /run/cdi/nvidia.yaml
    is fresh, and the CDI consumer loads nvidia.com/gpu exactly once (karr #11)
  - aarch64 / NVIDIA DGX Spark (GB10) support, part 1 (detection + RHEL repo)
    and part 2 (Ubuntu open-kernel-module driver selection). Detection
    classifies the GB10 (PCI 10de:2e12) as compute via a known-compute
    device-ID allowlist: it enumerates as a VGA controller [0300] and, on a
    host whose pci.ids predates the silicon, lspci prints only "Device" with
    no marketing name, so the name-token rules classified it compute => 0 and
    gpu_setup skipped the whole pipeline on a Spark (verified live on cortex).
    The allowlist is positive-only and does not change the unknown-model
    default (still 0). The RHEL/Rocky/Alma CUDA repository URL is
    architecture-aware: aarch64 uses the "sbsa" tree (repos/rhelN/sbsa/),
    x86_64 is unchanged — the old hardcoded x86_64 pointed aarch64 at a
    nonexistent repo. gpu_setup now threads the detected GPU (name + PCI
    device ID) through to install_driver's new optional gpu option; on
    Ubuntu, on x86_64 and aarch64 alike, a Blackwell-architecture GPU
    (B200/GB200/B300, GeForce RTX 50xx, RTX PRO Blackwell, GB10 — none has a
    proprietary kernel module) now gets the -server-open package variant
    instead of the default -server one, which the previous auto-detect
    explicitly filtered *-open* candidates OUT of; before, an RTX 50xx or
    B200 on x86_64 got a -server driver dpkg reports installed but whose
    module never binds. Blackwell is recognised by PCI device ID (0x2900-
    0x2FFF plus B300/GB300 3182, 31C2-31C3, per NVIDIA's open-gpu-kernel-
    modules supported-GPU table); every other GPU, including an unknown
    future ID, picks -server exactly as before (karr #16). New pure,
    offline-testable predicates
    Rex::GPU::Detect::open_kernel_module_required and
    Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::_ubuntu_needs_open_kernel_module drive the decision
    (new t/80-ubuntu-open-driver.t); the -server-open package name was
    confirmed published for arm64 against Ubuntu's Launchpad archive, not
    against a live apt-cache. NB: NEITHER the RHEL aarch64 install NOR this
    Ubuntu -open install has run on real hardware — cortex (the only GB10
    host) already has its driver loaded (DGX-OS prebuilt), so the karr #10
    idempotency short-circuit skips install_driver there entirely, and no
    fresh Ubuntu/RHEL aarch64 Spark was available, nor any x86_64 Blackwell
    host; a maintainer must confirm both against one (karr #14/#15/#16)
  - Make install_driver idempotent against a host that already has a working
    NVIDIA driver (e.g. provisioned via the NVIDIA CUDA apt/dnf repo, or a
    re-run of gpu_setup): a distro-neutral short-circuit now skips the driver
    install when nvidia-smi -L already lists a GPU, before the per-distro
    package selection. Previously the Ubuntu branch auto-selected a versioned
    nvidia-driver-NNN-server whose libs Conflict with the installed
    (unversioned) userspace, so apt refused and gpu_setup died at the
    install-verify seam; the auto-detected version could also be LOWER than the
    installed one (a downgrade). The nouveau blacklist and reboot are skipped
    too, so the call is a clean no-op on an already-provisioned host (karr #10)
  - Fix containerd config for RKE2/K3s clobbering the distribution's native
    config on modern hosts. The old code wrote a full config.toml.tmpl
    (version=2, io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri) that REPLACED the RKE2/K3s-generated
    config — on containerd 2.x / config v3 that dropped SystemdCgroup, the
    pinned sandbox image, snapshotter opts and the certs.d config_path.
    configure_containerd is now version-aware and additive: a no-op when
    RKE2/K3s already wired the nvidia runtime natively, a config-v3.toml.d/
    drop-in on modern hosts, or a base-extending config.toml.tmpl (renders the
    distribution base first via its "base" template, then adds nvidia) on
    legacy config-v2 hosts. Handles RKE2 and K3s under their respective
    /var/lib/rancher/*/agent/etc/containerd/ dirs (karr #9)
  - Fix openSUSE Leap NVIDIA driver: operating_system_version() strips dots
    ("15.6" -> "156"), so int() routed *every* Leap through the >= 16 branch
    and Leap 15.x got the leap/16.0/ repo + G07 meta package instead of
    leap/15.x/ + G06. Derive the major from the raw operating_system_release()
    string; add offline unit test t/20-suse-driver.t (karr #6)
  - Document actual detected NVIDIA name form in the SYNOPSIS examples
    ("AD104GL [RTX 4000 SFF Ada Generation]", codename + bracketed marketing
    string) — the previous "NVIDIA RTX ..." example did not match the code (karr #5)
  - Rex::LibSSH >= 0.004 host-key verification: the eg/ deploy examples now
    KEEP verification on and ssh-keyscan the fresh host into known_hosts via a
    'before ALL' pre-connect hook (rancher_scan_known_hosts), consistent with
    Rex::Rancher and this dist's own POD recommendation. Override by adding
    disable_strict_host_key_checking to the feature list.
  - Mark openSUSE Leap / SLES as unverified and unsupported (POD + README):
    SUSE is not a deploy target for the GPU-on-Rancher pipeline; the verified
    set is the RKE2 Linux family (Debian/Ubuntu/RHEL/Rocky/Alma). The
    _install_driver_suse code stays but is no longer advertised as supported,
    consistent with Rex::Rancher's node-prep matrix.

0.001     2026-03-29 04:20:30Z
  - Initial release
  - GPU hardware detection via PCI class codes (NVIDIA, AMD)
  - NVIDIA driver installation (Debian 12/13, Ubuntu 22.04/24.04, RHEL/Rocky 8/9/10, openSUSE Leap 15.6/16.0)
  - NVIDIA Container Toolkit installation with automatic repo setup
  - CDI spec generation via nvidia-ctk cdi generate
  - Containerd runtime configuration (RKE2, K3s, standalone)
  - Support for Ubuntu 24.04: auto-detect nvidia-driver-NNN-server, handle virtual nvidia-smi package
  - DPkg::Lock::Timeout=120 on all apt-get calls for resilience on fresh-boot systems
  - All package installs use apt-get/dnf directly with dpkg/rpm verification
    (Rex::Pkg dies on non-zero from DKMS/grub/initramfs post-install scripts)
  - Reboot support with reconnect polling via Rex::LibSSH
  - Tested on Hetzner dedicated servers with NVIDIA RTX 4000 SFF Ada Generation
    (Debian 13, Rocky Linux 10.1, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS)
  - eg/Rexfile example for GPU detection and cluster deployment
  - Requires Rex::LibSSH for deployment to SFTP-less hosts

